WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.460
&gt;&gt; Sandy Blythe.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:16.565
&gt;&gt; Hey folks, come on
down. Welcome back.

00:00:16.566 --> 00:00:18.465
Hope you all have had

00:00:18.466 --> 00:00:21.960
a great first day
of Faculty Summit.

00:00:21.961 --> 00:00:25.630
I should thank of course
first and foremost,

00:00:25.631 --> 00:00:28.995
the speakers from
our breakout sessions.

00:00:28.996 --> 00:00:30.600
Especially, our
university partners

00:00:30.601 --> 00:00:31.860
who presented with us.

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:33.990
I thought the topics
were top-notch

00:00:33.991 --> 00:00:36.210
and I very much appreciate all of

00:00:36.211 --> 00:00:38.340
the speakers for
the evident care and

00:00:38.341 --> 00:00:41.125
effort that they put into
their presentations.

00:00:41.126 --> 00:00:42.710
So, thank you to all of the

00:00:42.711 --> 00:00:45.680
breakout speakers,we've
got more to come.

00:00:45.681 --> 00:00:48.975
I'm particularly excited
about the next topic.

00:00:48.976 --> 00:00:50.385
As I said this morning,

00:00:50.386 --> 00:00:52.820
I thought that was
an amazing canvas

00:00:52.821 --> 00:00:54.470
of opportunity in
Systems Research,

00:00:54.471 --> 00:00:57.290
and it's not just for great
research and instruction.

00:00:57.291 --> 00:00:58.370
I think there's an opportunity

00:00:58.371 --> 00:00:59.975
for entrepreneurship as well.

00:00:59.976 --> 00:01:03.620
We've got a panel of people
who have had success in

00:01:03.621 --> 00:01:06.080
translating
their Systems Research

00:01:06.081 --> 00:01:08.210
into entrepreneurial success,

00:01:08.211 --> 00:01:09.800
and I'm very pleased to introduce

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:11.120
our first panelist from

00:01:11.121 --> 00:01:13.905
Microsoft Research,
Ranveer Chandra.

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:16.940
Ranveer has a career

00:01:16.941 --> 00:01:19.300
at Microsoft as
a Principal Researcher.

00:01:19.301 --> 00:01:22.730
He's received himself
over 85 patents

00:01:22.731 --> 00:01:24.790
here in the US and
more on the way.

00:01:24.791 --> 00:01:27.200
He's been running
Battery projects

00:01:27.201 --> 00:01:29.315
and TV white spaces project.

00:01:29.316 --> 00:01:31.130
He's probably best
known at the moment for

00:01:31.131 --> 00:01:32.960
running a project
called FarmBeats,

00:01:32.961 --> 00:01:36.420
which is really an internal
start up at Microsoft.

00:01:36.421 --> 00:01:38.300
It started as a
research project in

00:01:38.301 --> 00:01:41.520
IOT for digital agronomy,

00:01:41.521 --> 00:01:45.070
and has moved now out
into customers' hands.

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:46.400
It is making a difference

00:01:46.401 --> 00:01:48.890
in reducing the cost
and increasing

00:01:48.891 --> 00:01:50.870
the viability of agriculture as

00:01:50.871 --> 00:01:53.825
we face a future of
increasing scarcity.

00:01:53.826 --> 00:01:56.010
He's been well-recognized,

00:01:56.011 --> 00:01:58.060
published in a number of venues,

00:01:58.061 --> 00:02:00.115
The Economists,
The Wall Street Journal.

00:02:00.116 --> 00:02:03.265
He's an MIT TR35,

00:02:03.266 --> 00:02:06.570
the technology TR35 winner
as a top innovator.

00:02:06.571 --> 00:02:09.590
So, really has a lot to say about

00:02:09.591 --> 00:02:12.960
entrepreneurship along
with his two co-panelists.

00:02:12.961 --> 00:02:15.525
So with that, let me
introduce Ranveer.

00:02:15.526 --> 00:02:18.630
Here you go. Thank you.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.500
&gt;&gt; Thank you Sandy and
thank you all for coming.

00:02:22.501 --> 00:02:25.010
Today we have
an exciting 35 minutes,

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.650
where we have two
distinguished panelists.

00:02:27.651 --> 00:02:29.340
We'll be discussing about

00:02:29.341 --> 00:02:32.020
Systems Research and
Entrepreneurship.

00:02:32.021 --> 00:02:34.615
The two distinguished
professors we have,

00:02:34.616 --> 00:02:36.125
one is Dawn Song,

00:02:36.126 --> 00:02:38.930
from UC Berkeley, and Matei from

00:02:38.931 --> 00:02:43.780
Stanford University.
Welcome Dawn.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:45.720
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:02:45.721 --> 00:02:46.050
&gt;&gt; Welcome Matei.

00:02:46.051 --> 00:02:46.560
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:02:46.561 --> 00:02:48.670
&gt;&gt; So both Dawn and Matei,

00:02:48.671 --> 00:02:51.825
are very well known faculty.

00:02:51.826 --> 00:02:55.055
Dawn is a professor
at UC Berkeley.

00:02:55.056 --> 00:02:57.375
Her papers are very well cited.

00:02:57.376 --> 00:02:59.130
In addition to that,
she's also received

00:02:59.131 --> 00:03:00.940
multiple awards,
the MacArthur Fellowship,

00:03:00.941 --> 00:03:03.190
the Sloan Fellowship,
the MIT TR-35 Award,

00:03:03.191 --> 00:03:05.245
and the list goes on and
on, and we won't go there.

00:03:05.246 --> 00:03:07.745
For this session, what's
exciting is that she's done

00:03:07.746 --> 00:03:12.250
multiple startups Insighter
Security sold to Fire I.

00:03:12.251 --> 00:03:13.760
The other one Mendel Security,

00:03:13.761 --> 00:03:15.040
and the third one
which she's doing

00:03:15.041 --> 00:03:16.610
right now is on Oasis Labs.

00:03:16.611 --> 00:03:18.930
She does research in
Security, Blockchain,

00:03:18.931 --> 00:03:21.865
Machine Learning, AI,
Cloud, everything systems.

00:03:21.866 --> 00:03:25.305
Then Matei from
Stanford University.

00:03:25.306 --> 00:03:27.950
Matei is a young faculty there.

00:03:27.951 --> 00:03:29.340
He's already done amazing work.

00:03:29.341 --> 00:03:30.710
His PhD thesis was among the

00:03:30.711 --> 00:03:32.600
best read in recent times which

00:03:32.601 --> 00:03:36.960
was on Apache Spark,

00:03:36.961 --> 00:03:38.150
which led to Databricks.

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:41.240
So he released his code
that started as part of

00:03:41.241 --> 00:03:42.755
a PhD thesis which received

00:03:42.756 --> 00:03:44.995
the ACM Best Dissertation Award,

00:03:44.996 --> 00:03:46.700
this was at least as open source.

00:03:46.701 --> 00:03:48.320
Since then, he's actually

00:03:48.321 --> 00:03:50.200
founded the startup which
is doing super well,

00:03:50.201 --> 00:03:53.680
which has raised
over $200 million,

00:03:53.681 --> 00:03:55.470
and has several customers,

00:03:55.471 --> 00:03:57.530
and it's on Azure as well.

00:03:57.531 --> 00:04:00.995
It's one of the course
along with Databricks.

00:04:00.996 --> 00:04:04.455
So, welcome again for
this exciting discussion.

00:04:04.456 --> 00:04:07.920
So one of the things as
systems researchers,

00:04:07.921 --> 00:04:11.620
we run into is all of
us who do research,

00:04:11.621 --> 00:04:15.365
want to build systems that
is used by a lot of people.

00:04:15.366 --> 00:04:16.610
We want it to scale out,

00:04:16.611 --> 00:04:19.340
we want it to be adopted
out there in the world.

00:04:19.341 --> 00:04:21.380
There are multiple
ways to do that.

00:04:21.381 --> 00:04:23.610
All of us at some point think
that, "Hey, you know what?

00:04:23.611 --> 00:04:25.650
It's ready, we are ready
to take this out."

00:04:25.651 --> 00:04:27.890
The few ways you can
actually take it out to

00:04:27.891 --> 00:04:29.150
multiple people is either you can

00:04:29.151 --> 00:04:30.725
release it as open source.

00:04:30.726 --> 00:04:34.220
My adviser Ken Burman had
recently written blog on that,

00:04:34.221 --> 00:04:36.195
how do you venture
to Open-Source,

00:04:36.196 --> 00:04:38.355
your solutions either Open-Source

00:04:38.356 --> 00:04:39.680
or you could license it out.

00:04:39.681 --> 00:04:42.050
There various faculty
who've done very well by

00:04:42.051 --> 00:04:43.410
licensing out the technology like

00:04:43.411 --> 00:04:45.565
Guri Sohi from Wisconsin.

00:04:45.566 --> 00:04:47.510
Or the third, which
both of you have

00:04:47.511 --> 00:04:49.515
done which is to do a startup.

00:04:49.516 --> 00:04:52.150
So, what made you decide,

00:04:52.151 --> 00:04:53.870
don't worry I have to first
ask you this question since

00:04:53.871 --> 00:04:56.045
you've done one star
developed another.

00:04:56.046 --> 00:04:57.840
When did you decide that

00:04:57.841 --> 00:04:59.845
you are ready to do a startup and

00:04:59.846 --> 00:05:01.910
why start-up why not license it

00:05:01.911 --> 00:05:05.050
out or put your code
out as Open-Source?

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.070
&gt;&gt; So different researchers have

00:05:07.071 --> 00:05:08.780
different preferences and for

00:05:08.781 --> 00:05:11.960
me I was feeling
that as a researcher

00:05:11.961 --> 00:05:14.165
not only did I want to build

00:05:14.166 --> 00:05:16.685
new technologies and
really excited about

00:05:16.686 --> 00:05:20.360
the innovation from
the technical perspective

00:05:20.361 --> 00:05:21.785
and also at the same time

00:05:21.786 --> 00:05:24.290
I feel as a researcher
often we do

00:05:24.291 --> 00:05:27.080
have a responsibility
to take our creations,

00:05:27.081 --> 00:05:28.850
our new technologies into

00:05:28.851 --> 00:05:31.410
the real world to help
change the world,

00:05:31.411 --> 00:05:33.445
to help improve the world.

00:05:33.446 --> 00:05:40.085
With that I think I enjoy
the process of seeing and also

00:05:40.086 --> 00:05:43.490
the journey of taking the
technology that we developed

00:05:43.491 --> 00:05:47.390
in the lab and actually into
the real world to see it.

00:05:47.391 --> 00:05:52.640
Also that's a great way as
a first hence and you can

00:05:52.641 --> 00:05:55.580
see out what needs
to be improved in

00:05:55.581 --> 00:05:56.600
the technology that you have

00:05:56.601 --> 00:05:58.990
developed and how to
make it even better.

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:02.360
I think all these are great
research to actually do it to

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:06.099
faster hands and also as
technology innovators.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.070
Oftentimes, you are the one who

00:06:08.071 --> 00:06:10.265
actually understands
the technology best

00:06:10.266 --> 00:06:12.110
and hence you know
what's the best way

00:06:12.111 --> 00:06:14.750
to adjust and adapt
to the real world,

00:06:14.751 --> 00:06:17.825
use cases and to find the
best product market fits.

00:06:17.826 --> 00:06:19.190
&gt;&gt; So, this is a good answer.

00:06:19.191 --> 00:06:21.830
This is something which
propels me as well

00:06:21.831 --> 00:06:24.970
doing PVC with TV white spaces
all with FarmBeats.

00:06:24.971 --> 00:06:26.720
Success is not just
writing a paper,

00:06:26.721 --> 00:06:29.165
it's actually getting it out
into the hands of everyone.

00:06:29.166 --> 00:06:31.160
This is a different principle
for some faculty it

00:06:31.161 --> 00:06:31.900
could just be published

00:06:31.901 --> 00:06:33.390
the paper someone
will pick it up.

00:06:33.391 --> 00:06:35.560
So, Matei you've done both.

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:36.520
You released your code as

00:06:36.521 --> 00:06:38.115
Open-Source which
has been a big hit

00:06:38.116 --> 00:06:39.320
and you're also doing

00:06:39.321 --> 00:06:41.070
a startup which is
another big success.

00:06:41.071 --> 00:06:42.650
So, how did you decide but each

00:06:42.651 --> 00:06:44.480
one of them which route to go?

00:06:44.481 --> 00:06:47.725
And would your decision have
changed like with Spark?

00:06:47.726 --> 00:06:49.640
Would you still have released
it as Open-Source of

00:06:49.641 --> 00:06:52.110
back then you knew that
you were doing a startup?

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:53.640
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Good question yeah.

00:06:53.641 --> 00:06:56.105
So, in general releasing

00:06:56.106 --> 00:06:59.240
software as Open-Source from
research is a great way

00:06:59.241 --> 00:07:02.645
to see whether people
can actually use it and

00:07:02.646 --> 00:07:04.700
also engage with early users who

00:07:04.701 --> 00:07:06.830
are willing to try it out
and give you feedback.

00:07:06.831 --> 00:07:08.630
So, that was great.

00:07:08.631 --> 00:07:11.960
I think for us one reason to

00:07:11.961 --> 00:07:13.760
start a company around
it was actually

00:07:13.761 --> 00:07:15.750
just to push it to
the next level.

00:07:15.751 --> 00:07:19.160
So, Apache Spark
already had a bunch of

00:07:19.161 --> 00:07:20.770
early users when we

00:07:20.771 --> 00:07:23.750
decided to start the company
and it had some traction.

00:07:23.751 --> 00:07:25.200
People were excited about it,

00:07:25.201 --> 00:07:26.960
but in a university you have

00:07:26.961 --> 00:07:29.290
limited resources to work on it.

00:07:29.291 --> 00:07:31.820
So, we felt that as a company we

00:07:31.821 --> 00:07:34.760
could actually invest
a lot more in it,

00:07:34.761 --> 00:07:36.950
push it to the next
level and tackle

00:07:36.951 --> 00:07:39.320
a whole new set of
problems with it.

00:07:39.321 --> 00:07:41.240
That's exactly what
happened actually

00:07:41.241 --> 00:07:43.510
since we started the company,

00:07:43.511 --> 00:07:45.640
both what's in the software,

00:07:45.641 --> 00:07:48.215
like the functionality
and features have

00:07:48.216 --> 00:07:51.140
grown tremendously and also
the developer community

00:07:51.141 --> 00:07:52.640
has grown tremendously and it's

00:07:52.641 --> 00:07:55.370
definitely partly because
the company spent a lot of

00:07:55.371 --> 00:07:57.515
time making sure it's super easy

00:07:57.516 --> 00:07:59.885
for people to use and
to be successful with.

00:07:59.886 --> 00:08:01.250
&gt;&gt; So, basically
what you're saying

00:08:01.251 --> 00:08:02.860
is depending on your idea.

00:08:02.861 --> 00:08:04.215
Depending on what you're doing,

00:08:04.216 --> 00:08:06.605
you might want in some cases
to Open-Source it.

00:08:06.606 --> 00:08:07.220
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:08:07.221 --> 00:08:08.840
&gt;&gt; Do you even for
example you created

00:08:08.841 --> 00:08:10.260
courses that are
on some of these.

00:08:10.261 --> 00:08:13.720
And you have so many people
trained to use Apache Spark,

00:08:13.721 --> 00:08:16.100
and how did that lead to
the success of Databrcks?

00:08:16.101 --> 00:08:18.065
&gt;&gt; Yeah. That's a thing
that company did to

00:08:18.066 --> 00:08:20.810
promote it and to
help people use it.

00:08:20.811 --> 00:08:22.880
But if it is partly a matter

00:08:22.881 --> 00:08:25.070
of scale as I said and it
depends on this after.

00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:27.140
I mean this was basically

00:08:27.141 --> 00:08:30.105
a distributed computing platform
and standard library

00:08:30.106 --> 00:08:32.480
every data processing algorithm

00:08:32.481 --> 00:08:35.760
you want to use giving you
a distributed version of it.

00:08:35.761 --> 00:08:37.060
That's a pretty big effort.

00:08:37.061 --> 00:08:39.530
There are some other for
it maybe you just build

00:08:39.531 --> 00:08:41.450
a small Open-Source project

00:08:41.451 --> 00:08:42.700
and it's going to be that way.

00:08:42.701 --> 00:08:44.745
&gt;&gt; All right. That's
a good point.

00:08:44.746 --> 00:08:47.505
So, the other question which
is related to this is,

00:08:47.506 --> 00:08:49.699
how did you do that transition

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:51.734
once you wanted to
do this startup,

00:08:51.735 --> 00:08:53.030
how did you go about doing it?

00:08:53.031 --> 00:08:55.010
Did you- in some cases,

00:08:55.011 --> 00:08:56.750
some academics are good
at doing research,

00:08:56.751 --> 00:08:59.880
do you hire someone from
outside to be the CEO?

00:08:59.881 --> 00:09:02.025
Run with it? Do you
do it yourself?

00:09:02.026 --> 00:09:03.400
How do you build a team?

00:09:03.401 --> 00:09:05.620
At what point, because
you still are students,

00:09:05.621 --> 00:09:07.970
at what point do you
decide to take it

00:09:07.971 --> 00:09:10.770
out versus just keep still
doing research on top,

00:09:10.771 --> 00:09:13.410
building something solid
and how do you take it out?

00:09:13.411 --> 00:09:15.570
How do you make it
into a startup?

00:09:15.571 --> 00:09:16.620
When do you in cooperate?

00:09:16.621 --> 00:09:17.970
When do you build a
startup out of it?

00:09:17.971 --> 00:09:19.165
Dawn, why don't you start with

00:09:19.166 --> 00:09:21.430
three experiences you
had, one after another.

00:09:21.431 --> 00:09:24.565
You could start with the
first one in cyber security.

00:09:24.566 --> 00:09:26.195
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Given that I've done

00:09:26.196 --> 00:09:27.710
two and then now I'm
doing the third.

00:09:27.711 --> 00:09:34.005
So, I do have a very broad
experience in this regard.

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:36.015
So, in terms of
whether we should hire

00:09:36.016 --> 00:09:37.845
outside CEO or bringing

00:09:37.846 --> 00:09:40.710
outside CEO and whether we
should do it ourselves.

00:09:40.711 --> 00:09:43.785
So, in my case actually
we've done both.

00:09:43.786 --> 00:09:46.290
For my first company as well as

00:09:46.291 --> 00:09:47.660
the third company the one

00:09:47.661 --> 00:09:49.415
that I'm doing right now,
always use the labs.

00:09:49.416 --> 00:09:52.635
So, I'm the CEO, the
founder and CEO.

00:09:52.636 --> 00:09:54.660
For the second company
Mendel security,

00:09:54.661 --> 00:09:57.080
so that one we actually
have a great CEO

00:09:57.081 --> 00:09:59.995
who has been running
it from very early on.

00:09:59.996 --> 00:10:02.040
I think in different cases,

00:10:02.041 --> 00:10:04.855
they actually both have
worked out really well.

00:10:04.856 --> 00:10:08.355
So in the first case and
also for the third one,

00:10:08.356 --> 00:10:10.715
so essentially these
are technologies.

00:10:10.716 --> 00:10:12.740
Again as I mentioned one thing is

00:10:12.741 --> 00:10:17.740
that as we develop
technologies, then.

00:10:17.741 --> 00:10:19.670
Besides the technology side,

00:10:19.671 --> 00:10:21.470
the biggest question is to

00:10:21.471 --> 00:10:24.019
find product market fits and also

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:27.500
the CEO's job is
precise on hiring and

00:10:27.501 --> 00:10:29.060
raising money is to

00:10:29.061 --> 00:10:31.330
be able to carry out
the overall vision,

00:10:31.331 --> 00:10:32.850
the direction for the company.

00:10:32.851 --> 00:10:37.595
So oftentimes, it's bit tricky
for us as CEO who don't

00:10:37.596 --> 00:10:39.380
understand the
technology well enough

00:10:39.381 --> 00:10:42.800
to actually meet the vision
and also to even

00:10:42.801 --> 00:10:44.660
find the good product market

00:10:44.661 --> 00:10:46.820
fits because they may
not know the technology

00:10:46.821 --> 00:10:48.755
enough to be able
to quickly figure

00:10:48.756 --> 00:10:51.980
out what may be
the best product market fit.

00:10:51.981 --> 00:10:54.335
So, in the first case
and the third case,

00:10:54.336 --> 00:10:59.270
so I was able to allow the
CEO and it was difficult to,

00:10:59.271 --> 00:11:01.895
I mean I was happy to do
it and also it's hard to

00:11:01.896 --> 00:11:06.020
find other CEOs to
actually to do this.

00:11:06.021 --> 00:11:09.740
I think we were very lucky
for the second case where,

00:11:09.741 --> 00:11:12.410
I think just through chances we

00:11:12.411 --> 00:11:15.170
were introduced
through friends and

00:11:15.171 --> 00:11:18.275
the CEO Amiae and he has

00:11:18.276 --> 00:11:20.210
a great track record and who was

00:11:20.211 --> 00:11:22.355
already very successful as
an entrepreneur before,

00:11:22.356 --> 00:11:25.865
who has done startup that
was acquired and so on.

00:11:25.866 --> 00:11:29.210
Also the things that
he actually already

00:11:29.211 --> 00:11:32.040
was passionate about
the space that we were in,

00:11:32.041 --> 00:11:33.480
the technology that
we were developing,

00:11:33.481 --> 00:11:35.355
he was actually
thinking even something

00:11:35.356 --> 00:11:36.750
similar in that domain.

00:11:36.751 --> 00:11:37.800
So, he already had

00:11:37.801 --> 00:11:39.480
a very good understanding of

00:11:39.481 --> 00:11:41.480
the space and his background,

00:11:41.481 --> 00:11:42.785
he has done a ton of

00:11:42.786 --> 00:11:45.755
enterprise sales in
his prior startups.

00:11:45.756 --> 00:11:47.960
So, his background
is a great fit in

00:11:47.961 --> 00:11:51.040
terms of really actually
helping to define

00:11:51.041 --> 00:11:53.360
the product market fit and
also to take the product

00:11:53.361 --> 00:11:57.245
really to enterprise
customers and really grow it.

00:11:57.246 --> 00:12:00.070
He's really excited and
really passionate and

00:12:00.071 --> 00:12:01.890
committed to take the technology

00:12:01.891 --> 00:12:03.350
in a fully going forward.

00:12:03.351 --> 00:12:04.955
&gt;&gt; Your involvement
is still there

00:12:04.956 --> 00:12:06.590
as the technologist or?

00:12:06.591 --> 00:12:08.940
In that particular case?

00:12:08.941 --> 00:12:13.355
&gt;&gt; Luckily, and so another
Berkeley grad students,

00:12:13.356 --> 00:12:15.079
we worked together
on this technology

00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:16.370
and he actually has been

00:12:16.371 --> 00:12:20.105
the co-founder and chief
architect of the technology,

00:12:20.106 --> 00:12:22.715
and he's still as a company.

00:12:22.716 --> 00:12:24.920
So, I think that really helps.

00:12:24.921 --> 00:12:27.310
&gt;&gt; That's a good perspective.
Matei yourself,

00:12:27.311 --> 00:12:30.540
you are still the chief
technologist at Databricks?

00:12:30.541 --> 00:12:31.660
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:12:31.661 --> 00:12:34.380
&gt;&gt; Your CEO is again internal,

00:12:34.381 --> 00:12:36.475
someone who was part
of the initial team?

00:12:36.476 --> 00:12:37.930
So how did you decide

00:12:37.931 --> 00:12:39.640
that experience mainly
in the context of

00:12:39.641 --> 00:12:43.685
the audience who are faculty,

00:12:43.686 --> 00:12:45.600
some of them might want
to become entrepreneurs,

00:12:45.601 --> 00:12:47.540
so even license that idea out or

00:12:47.541 --> 00:12:49.980
to hire people to take
their ideas to scale.

00:12:49.981 --> 00:12:51.630
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Well, I think again,

00:12:51.631 --> 00:12:53.360
I think the reason to do it

00:12:53.361 --> 00:12:55.515
yourself is if you
have a vision for it,

00:12:55.516 --> 00:12:58.280
maybe only you can
execute or can see

00:12:58.281 --> 00:13:01.170
and it's really
hard to get someone

00:13:01.171 --> 00:13:03.080
else to do it and especially

00:13:03.081 --> 00:13:05.360
also to hire someone who is very

00:13:05.361 --> 00:13:07.760
good to do that because maybe

00:13:07.761 --> 00:13:10.400
it hasn't been validated but
do you really believe in it.

00:13:10.401 --> 00:13:12.405
I think that's
the reason to do it.

00:13:12.406 --> 00:13:15.610
My experience was a little
different in a few ways,

00:13:15.611 --> 00:13:18.110
so one thing is I was
just finishing my PhD,

00:13:18.111 --> 00:13:21.600
so I didn't have my own
research group at the time.

00:13:21.601 --> 00:13:23.980
So, that made it easier
to take some time

00:13:23.981 --> 00:13:26.870
off completely and just
work on the startup.

00:13:26.871 --> 00:13:29.270
Also, we started with a bunch

00:13:29.271 --> 00:13:32.390
of co-founders actually from
the same research group,

00:13:32.391 --> 00:13:34.630
including my two advisers

00:13:34.631 --> 00:13:36.970
and a lot of students
and Ali Ghodsi,

00:13:36.971 --> 00:13:38.210
who is the CEO now,

00:13:38.211 --> 00:13:40.110
who was a visiting researcher

00:13:40.111 --> 00:13:41.860
working in distributed systems.

00:13:41.861 --> 00:13:45.180
So, we already had a pretty
tight-knit team that had

00:13:45.181 --> 00:13:49.295
worked together for years and
could work well together.

00:13:49.296 --> 00:13:51.300
We definitely learned
a lot of stuff,

00:13:51.301 --> 00:13:55.360
we had some people on
the team like Ali and Yance

00:13:55.361 --> 00:13:57.710
and Scott who had
startup experience and we

00:13:57.711 --> 00:14:00.090
had others like me
who didn't but,

00:14:00.091 --> 00:14:05.790
we had enough critical mass
to build things and

00:14:05.791 --> 00:14:08.870
to show promise of
our idea and to

00:14:08.871 --> 00:14:10.500
be able to hire the right people

00:14:10.501 --> 00:14:12.100
in the other roles
at the company.

00:14:12.101 --> 00:14:13.445
&gt;&gt; Wonderful. So, then

00:14:13.446 --> 00:14:15.320
about raising money which
is related to this,

00:14:15.321 --> 00:14:18.420
so when you're in the valley
but even then so a lot

00:14:18.421 --> 00:14:19.720
of startups that we see are

00:14:19.721 --> 00:14:21.810
either business
model innovations.

00:14:21.811 --> 00:14:23.970
These are the ones that
maybe in the social space,

00:14:23.971 --> 00:14:25.370
there's no technical
meet behind it

00:14:25.371 --> 00:14:27.020
but for most people
in this audience,

00:14:27.021 --> 00:14:30.120
these are really strong
technical contributions,

00:14:30.121 --> 00:14:32.195
papers in the top
tier conferences.

00:14:32.196 --> 00:14:34.560
Do you think these kind of
technical contributions

00:14:34.561 --> 00:14:38.170
have been going out to
VCs to raise money,

00:14:38.171 --> 00:14:41.735
do you see it making
it easier for you to

00:14:41.736 --> 00:14:45.190
get into this space to kick
off the startup process?

00:14:45.191 --> 00:14:47.445
In particular Dawn,
I wanted to ask you,

00:14:47.446 --> 00:14:49.310
we've been hearing a lot
of comments around,

00:14:49.311 --> 00:14:52.500
a lot of news articles around
women finding it hard,

00:14:52.501 --> 00:14:53.910
women entrepreneurs
finding it hard to

00:14:53.911 --> 00:14:55.840
raise money in the valley.

00:14:55.841 --> 00:14:58.110
Did you face any of that? Do
you have any recommendations

00:14:58.111 --> 00:14:59.820
for female faculty in

00:14:59.821 --> 00:15:01.790
the audience who would like
to become entrepreneurs?

00:15:01.791 --> 00:15:03.330
Both questions together, right?

00:15:03.331 --> 00:15:04.660
So, maybe it's easier for

00:15:04.661 --> 00:15:06.640
female faculty because
it's coming in with

00:15:06.641 --> 00:15:08.515
a technical background and

00:15:08.516 --> 00:15:10.580
some suggestions
that you might have.

00:15:10.581 --> 00:15:13.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I think it
definitely today,

00:15:13.471 --> 00:15:16.820
we do have advantage that
we are technologists,

00:15:16.821 --> 00:15:18.710
so we already have developed

00:15:18.711 --> 00:15:20.060
innovative technologies that

00:15:20.061 --> 00:15:21.425
usually when we started company,

00:15:21.426 --> 00:15:22.790
so we have three cases,

00:15:22.791 --> 00:15:24.950
there are technologies that

00:15:24.951 --> 00:15:28.295
me and my group has
developed in my lab

00:15:28.296 --> 00:15:31.310
and we see the potential
and then we are

00:15:31.311 --> 00:15:33.060
excited about the
technology transfer and

00:15:33.061 --> 00:15:35.005
then we decided to
do the company.

00:15:35.006 --> 00:15:36.295
So, in this case, unlike

00:15:36.296 --> 00:15:39.775
many and other entrepreneurs,
when we go out,

00:15:39.776 --> 00:15:41.440
we actually already have

00:15:41.441 --> 00:15:46.610
the initial technology that
have already been developed.

00:15:46.611 --> 00:15:48.705
So, that I think it definitely,

00:15:48.706 --> 00:15:51.020
both from fundraising perspective

00:15:51.021 --> 00:15:53.230
and also even from just
bringing up the team

00:15:53.231 --> 00:15:56.440
where they have a great team
and the co-team

00:15:56.441 --> 00:15:58.100
are really passionate and

00:15:58.101 --> 00:16:00.590
excited about the technology
that we have been building.

00:16:00.591 --> 00:16:03.230
In certain cases,
it take four years.

00:16:03.231 --> 00:16:05.180
So, I think that's a great start.

00:16:05.181 --> 00:16:09.350
That's like a greater start

00:16:09.351 --> 00:16:14.390
than many of the other types
of entrepreneurs, companies.

00:16:14.391 --> 00:16:16.865
As a women entrepreneur,

00:16:16.866 --> 00:16:19.370
I think so, first of all,

00:16:19.371 --> 00:16:24.380
so we're just labs we just
raised our funding recently,

00:16:24.381 --> 00:16:25.609
we made the announcement,

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:27.260
we raised 45 million.

00:16:27.261 --> 00:16:28.100
&gt;&gt; Congratulations.

00:16:28.101 --> 00:16:31.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Thank you and
we also raised it in

00:16:31.041 --> 00:16:34.490
a very short period of
time and we have top

00:16:34.491 --> 00:16:38.374
of the line like great investors

00:16:38.375 --> 00:16:42.980
including Ann Jason and
Exel and many other.

00:16:42.981 --> 00:16:43.550
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:16:43.551 --> 00:16:48.800
&gt;&gt; All right. Investors.
So, for me I think,

00:16:48.801 --> 00:16:53.720
I was a pretty smooth process

00:16:53.721 --> 00:16:55.330
and also in particular I

00:16:55.331 --> 00:16:56.790
think actually as
a woman entrepreneur,

00:16:56.791 --> 00:16:59.320
we do have certain
advantages too,

00:16:59.321 --> 00:17:01.600
in the sense that
they actually have

00:17:01.601 --> 00:17:06.095
quite a few friends
who are women like

00:17:06.096 --> 00:17:09.875
VCs who run their funds and also

00:17:09.876 --> 00:17:15.470
other male VCs who run funds.

00:17:15.471 --> 00:17:17.360
There are a number of
them who actually have

00:17:17.361 --> 00:17:21.485
strong intent and support
for women entrepreneurs.

00:17:21.486 --> 00:17:22.250
&gt;&gt; That's really good.

00:17:22.251 --> 00:17:24.560
&gt;&gt; So, fundraising, I
think I've been lucky

00:17:24.561 --> 00:17:27.740
that the people that I have seen,

00:17:27.741 --> 00:17:30.870
they have been really supportive
for women entrepreneurs.

00:17:30.871 --> 00:17:33.045
&gt;&gt; So, female faculty,
please reach out to Dawn

00:17:33.046 --> 00:17:35.950
for any recommendations
of who to reach to.

00:17:35.951 --> 00:17:37.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Happy to help.

00:17:37.901 --> 00:17:40.780
&gt;&gt; Matei, did you have
to do any fundraising

00:17:40.781 --> 00:17:43.330
yourself or it's usually
the CEO who does it,

00:17:43.331 --> 00:17:45.695
how often do you have
to face the investors?

00:17:45.696 --> 00:17:48.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah. All the co-founders were

00:17:48.011 --> 00:17:50.675
involved in it and especially
at the beginning, I think.

00:17:50.676 --> 00:17:53.490
But I think the people
who want to do it,

00:17:53.491 --> 00:17:54.940
I mean I'll say
a couple of things.

00:17:54.941 --> 00:17:57.375
So, one thing is
anything you can do that

00:17:57.376 --> 00:18:00.430
shows validation
of your technology

00:18:00.431 --> 00:18:02.510
as in someone would actually buy

00:18:02.511 --> 00:18:06.050
it or even use it
is super important.

00:18:06.051 --> 00:18:08.885
So, just having a paper
at a top conference,

00:18:08.886 --> 00:18:10.600
they don't really know
how to judge you, I mean,

00:18:10.601 --> 00:18:12.430
they'll be happy to
grab coffee with

00:18:12.431 --> 00:18:14.730
you and talk because
their job is to go

00:18:14.731 --> 00:18:16.770
talk to lots of
interesting people in

00:18:16.771 --> 00:18:19.760
the technology space but
they don't really know.

00:18:19.761 --> 00:18:20.910
But if you say, "Okay.

00:18:20.911 --> 00:18:22.130
There's someone using it."

00:18:22.131 --> 00:18:23.980
Maybe someone in your university,

00:18:23.981 --> 00:18:26.010
maybe someone who used
the opensource thing,

00:18:26.011 --> 00:18:28.100
and so on and they asked them and

00:18:28.101 --> 00:18:30.480
they find it's solving
a real problem,

00:18:30.481 --> 00:18:32.870
that makes it a lot
easier for them.

00:18:32.871 --> 00:18:34.315
So, for us, we had

00:18:34.316 --> 00:18:38.270
opensource Spark software
and it was very

00:18:38.271 --> 00:18:40.770
easy for the VCs to
see that it's useful

00:18:40.771 --> 00:18:42.360
because a lot of the portfolio

00:18:42.361 --> 00:18:43.670
companies we're already using it,

00:18:43.671 --> 00:18:45.530
because they were startups
who try out it's kind

00:18:45.531 --> 00:18:48.085
of crazy new technology,

00:18:48.086 --> 00:18:50.760
but that's what I would go for.

00:18:50.761 --> 00:18:53.490
Definitely, even if you

00:18:53.491 --> 00:18:56.365
don't know some some
of the top VCs,

00:18:56.366 --> 00:18:58.370
I'm sure you know
other faculty members

00:18:58.371 --> 00:19:00.060
who know them and you can get

00:19:00.061 --> 00:19:01.180
an introduction through them and

00:19:01.181 --> 00:19:02.350
I think that will also carry

00:19:02.351 --> 00:19:04.780
a lot of weight if
someone they know says,

00:19:04.781 --> 00:19:06.280
"Oh, you got to talk to so and so

00:19:06.281 --> 00:19:07.910
because I think they have
something interesting.".

00:19:07.911 --> 00:19:09.940
&gt;&gt; That's sweet feedback.
So, users are important.

00:19:09.941 --> 00:19:12.520
Do they have to be paying
or do they have to at

00:19:12.521 --> 00:19:15.905
least have paying potential
or just demo would be fine?

00:19:15.906 --> 00:19:18.950
&gt;&gt; Well, I think it's good
if they actually use it to

00:19:18.951 --> 00:19:22.660
do something valuable
and you could ask them,

00:19:22.661 --> 00:19:24.400
"Is this actually
valuable to you?"

00:19:24.401 --> 00:19:26.040
They don't have to
be paying but they

00:19:26.041 --> 00:19:27.665
have to think it's useful.

00:19:27.666 --> 00:19:29.445
&gt;&gt; Okay. Great feedback.

00:19:29.446 --> 00:19:31.090
So, yes, so a followup question,

00:19:31.091 --> 00:19:32.950
which I think would
be in the mind

00:19:32.951 --> 00:19:35.505
of many of the people
in the audiences.

00:19:35.506 --> 00:19:39.140
You guys are running
successful startups right now,

00:19:39.141 --> 00:19:41.315
both of them have raised
quite a bit of money.

00:19:41.316 --> 00:19:44.435
How has any of
this entrepreneurship

00:19:44.436 --> 00:19:46.595
affected your research career

00:19:46.596 --> 00:19:48.310
positively and negatively, right?

00:19:48.311 --> 00:19:50.390
So, both ways how has it made

00:19:50.391 --> 00:19:52.600
your research stronger or weaker?

00:19:52.601 --> 00:19:55.915
What your opinions?
Dawn, you could start,

00:19:55.916 --> 00:19:57.880
you've gone through it three
times, and you're tenured,

00:19:57.881 --> 00:19:59.100
you have everything
in your table,

00:19:59.101 --> 00:20:01.270
you can say whatever you want.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:08.380
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, in general the
answer of course is positive.

00:20:08.381 --> 00:20:10.959
I think, again, also people

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:13.360
do things differently
and there are

00:20:13.361 --> 00:20:16.240
different startups that I

00:20:16.241 --> 00:20:18.835
think it's a very broad spectrum.

00:20:18.836 --> 00:20:20.290
So, in my case,

00:20:20.291 --> 00:20:23.620
also because my research
area is really broad,

00:20:23.621 --> 00:20:25.240
and the three startups,

00:20:25.241 --> 00:20:27.575
they're actually in
very different areas.

00:20:27.576 --> 00:20:29.580
The first one was
in mobile security.

00:20:29.581 --> 00:20:31.980
The second one was
in secure browsing.

00:20:31.981 --> 00:20:35.530
Then the third one
is block chain.

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:42.430
Also overall, my research
is very broad.

00:20:42.431 --> 00:20:45.010
So none of these I would
say in my case and

00:20:45.011 --> 00:20:47.620
not necessarily the particular
technical things that I

00:20:47.621 --> 00:20:50.980
learned from one company
influenced the next one,

00:20:50.981 --> 00:20:52.405
because again as I mentioned,

00:20:52.406 --> 00:20:55.210
I enjoy working at
different areas.

00:20:55.211 --> 00:20:58.450
But what I would say as really
the experience that I have

00:20:58.451 --> 00:21:02.395
gained through doing
the startups that really,

00:21:02.396 --> 00:21:03.910
I think that's another reason

00:21:03.911 --> 00:21:05.830
that I actually enjoyed this.

00:21:05.831 --> 00:21:07.965
I view it's, again,

00:21:07.966 --> 00:21:10.275
I think many of us here
are growth minded.

00:21:10.276 --> 00:21:14.150
Like we are here to really
to grow, to become better,

00:21:14.151 --> 00:21:17.870
and I think being a professor
and being a researcher,

00:21:17.871 --> 00:21:20.315
that's a great way for
us to learn and grow.

00:21:20.316 --> 00:21:22.760
On the other hand, being
an entrepreneur is

00:21:22.761 --> 00:21:24.920
a great way to learn and

00:21:24.921 --> 00:21:27.205
grow and it's very
complimentary to what

00:21:27.206 --> 00:21:29.980
we typically do as
a researcher, as a faculty.

00:21:29.981 --> 00:21:32.380
Hence, for that, I
think in general,

00:21:32.381 --> 00:21:33.610
what I have benefited from

00:21:33.611 --> 00:21:36.690
the entrepreneurship experience
is that it really

00:21:36.691 --> 00:21:41.080
helps me to be stronger
in these other dimension,

00:21:41.081 --> 00:21:43.060
that normally we don't get to

00:21:43.061 --> 00:21:46.765
exercise our experience,
and overall,

00:21:46.766 --> 00:21:48.115
that does help provide

00:21:48.116 --> 00:21:50.020
a broader perspective and

00:21:50.021 --> 00:21:52.060
when I then choose
research topics,

00:21:52.061 --> 00:21:55.735
I want to work in research and

00:21:55.736 --> 00:22:00.550
also in terms of after being
more effective as well.

00:22:00.551 --> 00:22:02.750
&gt;&gt; Because this is a pattern
in the startups that you do.

00:22:02.751 --> 00:22:04.665
You start a new research team,

00:22:04.666 --> 00:22:07.070
get some publications
out, do a start-up,

00:22:07.071 --> 00:22:09.340
get it acquired or
something and then

00:22:09.341 --> 00:22:11.830
come back do another research
which is great,

00:22:11.831 --> 00:22:14.770
you are scaling up your your
research through startups.

00:22:14.771 --> 00:22:16.890
&gt;&gt; Middy, your response
to this question?

00:22:16.891 --> 00:22:18.830
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean I think
for me being part of

00:22:18.831 --> 00:22:21.240
a startup has been
incredibly helpful and

00:22:21.241 --> 00:22:23.830
seen real-world problems as

00:22:23.831 --> 00:22:26.590
they arise and basically
finding new research areas

00:22:26.591 --> 00:22:29.260
that I know are important
because I've experienced

00:22:29.261 --> 00:22:30.810
the problem more
like our customers

00:22:30.811 --> 00:22:32.170
have experienced the problem,

00:22:32.171 --> 00:22:34.770
but that are maybe
harder for people to

00:22:34.771 --> 00:22:37.350
see or to even
understand in academia.

00:22:37.351 --> 00:22:39.760
Some of them, you
probably can't do at all

00:22:39.761 --> 00:22:42.964
unless you actually
work with customers.

00:22:42.965 --> 00:22:46.880
So, and these can be
very general problems,

00:22:46.881 --> 00:22:48.130
you should often not be

00:22:48.131 --> 00:22:50.180
specific to
a particular customer.

00:22:50.181 --> 00:22:51.770
So just as an example,

00:22:51.771 --> 00:22:55.570
database sells
only a cloud product.

00:22:55.571 --> 00:22:57.440
It's basically
software as a service

00:22:57.441 --> 00:22:59.910
on Amazon Web Services or Azure

00:22:59.911 --> 00:23:02.130
and the vast majority
of the revenue is just

00:23:02.131 --> 00:23:04.620
licensing fees of
using the software.

00:23:04.621 --> 00:23:07.020
It's not support or
consulting or anything.

00:23:07.021 --> 00:23:09.360
So it's got to build
a really good software

00:23:09.361 --> 00:23:11.020
as a service cloud product,

00:23:11.021 --> 00:23:12.940
that small [inaudible] ,

00:23:12.941 --> 00:23:16.010
secure and performer
and highly available,

00:23:16.011 --> 00:23:17.500
and building that is really

00:23:17.501 --> 00:23:19.405
hard and this is actually the way

00:23:19.406 --> 00:23:20.610
the vast majority of

00:23:20.611 --> 00:23:23.870
new enterprise software today
and even consumer software,

00:23:23.871 --> 00:23:26.050
software as a service
and I don't know

00:23:26.051 --> 00:23:28.680
any university class that
teaches you how to build it.

00:23:28.681 --> 00:23:31.690
I don't see any papers
at conferences that

00:23:31.691 --> 00:23:32.770
actually say here are

00:23:32.771 --> 00:23:34.710
the problems in terms
of building it,

00:23:34.711 --> 00:23:36.580
or like here's
our new abstraction that you

00:23:36.581 --> 00:23:39.095
can use to deploy
SaaS or whatever.

00:23:39.096 --> 00:23:42.100
It's just an interesting thing
to think about if

00:23:42.101 --> 00:23:44.000
people are going to keep building

00:23:44.001 --> 00:23:45.980
computer systems in
the future this way,

00:23:45.981 --> 00:23:47.710
we'd better understand
how to do it.

00:23:47.711 --> 00:23:50.200
So a lot of the kind
of problems I

00:23:50.201 --> 00:23:52.690
think about in research
are inspired by that,

00:23:52.691 --> 00:23:55.430
while you see something was
super hard like it literally

00:23:55.431 --> 00:23:58.240
took three years to get
right for database.

00:23:58.241 --> 00:23:59.840
It's got to be hard
for anyone else.

00:23:59.841 --> 00:24:02.245
Is there any way to
make that easier?

00:24:02.246 --> 00:24:03.960
&gt;&gt; This is great, so essentially

00:24:03.961 --> 00:24:05.020
what you're seeing is that

00:24:05.021 --> 00:24:07.070
both the startup
experiences helping

00:24:07.071 --> 00:24:10.140
enrich your research career.

00:24:10.141 --> 00:24:13.510
So I'll bring up
the negative part of it.

00:24:13.511 --> 00:24:16.220
So this is slightly
controversial, well,

00:24:16.221 --> 00:24:19.260
but in a positive way
that one of the questions

00:24:19.261 --> 00:24:22.565
I asked both Middy and
Dawn internally was, well,

00:24:22.566 --> 00:24:25.010
it's a full-time job
running a startup

00:24:25.011 --> 00:24:28.085
being I'm doing
being in academia,

00:24:28.086 --> 00:24:29.830
how do you manage it more
than with like well we

00:24:29.831 --> 00:24:32.080
spend 80 percent of
the time doing a startup,

00:24:32.081 --> 00:24:34.060
80 percent of the doing academia.

00:24:34.061 --> 00:24:36.120
So that's the time
we'd have zero time.

00:24:36.121 --> 00:24:38.100
So my question is more around,

00:24:38.101 --> 00:24:40.845
how do you manage
time for yourself,

00:24:40.846 --> 00:24:42.780
not about researcher startups,

00:24:42.781 --> 00:24:46.235
what is your me time and what
do you do in your me time?

00:24:46.236 --> 00:24:48.970
Any tips for people
who want to do both,

00:24:48.971 --> 00:24:50.870
and still have a me time?

00:24:50.871 --> 00:24:53.315
Dawn, why don't you start?

00:24:53.316 --> 00:24:55.005
&gt;&gt; That's a very good question,

00:24:55.006 --> 00:24:58.030
I wish there was
a great answer to it,

00:24:58.031 --> 00:25:01.285
because I asked
the same question and I think

00:25:01.286 --> 00:25:06.520
especially so I'm facing of
right now with our startups.

00:25:06.521 --> 00:25:08.080
We are growing very fast

00:25:08.081 --> 00:25:11.620
and and there's
a lot of examines,

00:25:11.621 --> 00:25:13.180
and we're talking to actually

00:25:13.181 --> 00:25:14.995
a lot of potential
customers as well,

00:25:14.996 --> 00:25:19.000
and there's just so much to do,

00:25:19.001 --> 00:25:21.410
and that they just.

00:25:21.411 --> 00:25:24.820
&gt;&gt; What do you do outside
of work? How do you relax?

00:25:24.821 --> 00:25:28.480
&gt;&gt; Actually, so earlier,

00:25:28.481 --> 00:25:31.495
I was taking fencing lessons.

00:25:31.496 --> 00:25:33.610
&gt;&gt; That helps, that
helps even with me.

00:25:33.611 --> 00:25:38.230
&gt;&gt; No, I just

00:25:38.231 --> 00:25:44.845
figured somehow I actually
enjoyed fencing as a sport,

00:25:44.846 --> 00:25:47.740
and then weekends
actually I do go

00:25:47.741 --> 00:25:50.935
running on the beach
when I have time.

00:25:50.936 --> 00:25:52.720
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Middy, any
tips from your side?

00:25:52.721 --> 00:25:55.250
I know it's even busier
for you, eight students,

00:25:55.251 --> 00:25:56.960
managing the startups,

00:25:56.961 --> 00:25:58.810
doing writing really
quality papers,

00:25:58.811 --> 00:25:59.855
you have been very prolific.

00:25:59.856 --> 00:26:01.525
&gt;&gt; I think Dawn has
a lot of students too.

00:26:01.526 --> 00:26:03.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah, she has
eight students as well,

00:26:03.041 --> 00:26:05.410
she fences with them and

00:26:05.411 --> 00:26:07.495
they are very loose.
What do you do?

00:26:07.496 --> 00:26:11.400
&gt;&gt; I mean my feeling on
this is even just academic

00:26:11.401 --> 00:26:13.340
job alone or just a startup job

00:26:13.341 --> 00:26:15.390
alone can easily
consume all your time.

00:26:15.391 --> 00:26:16.570
So, in a sense,

00:26:16.571 --> 00:26:18.905
you'd have the same problem
in either case,

00:26:18.906 --> 00:26:20.620
and you have to,

00:26:20.621 --> 00:26:23.700
in both cases, you have to
be mindful of your time,

00:26:23.701 --> 00:26:27.200
understand what's the best way
for you to spend stuff,

00:26:27.201 --> 00:26:29.615
when should you take time
off to do something else,

00:26:29.616 --> 00:26:31.400
when should you delegate,

00:26:31.401 --> 00:26:33.830
when you should evaluate
what you're doing,

00:26:33.831 --> 00:26:36.600
seeing is this actually
the right way to spend my time?

00:26:36.601 --> 00:26:39.205
What's actually most
important to spend time on?

00:26:39.206 --> 00:26:42.135
So I think it's not that they
are different, but you do,

00:26:42.136 --> 00:26:43.775
you have to expect that

00:26:43.776 --> 00:26:45.590
things will be
somewhat hectic and I

00:26:45.591 --> 00:26:47.135
think anyone who does either of

00:26:47.136 --> 00:26:49.020
these two jobs
really enjoys them.

00:26:49.021 --> 00:26:51.525
I don't think anyone
starts a tenure-track,

00:26:51.526 --> 00:26:53.745
faculty job without
enjoying data,

00:26:53.746 --> 00:26:54.930
or a startup for instance.

00:26:54.931 --> 00:26:56.380
&gt;&gt; So this is the
same in a research

00:26:56.381 --> 00:26:58.400
lab and sitting here
and telling you.

00:26:58.401 --> 00:27:00.660
We're here, we do it,
we work extra hours.

00:27:00.661 --> 00:27:02.570
It's because we enjoy doing what

00:27:02.571 --> 00:27:04.740
we do with any of these
[inaudible] project is about.

00:27:04.741 --> 00:27:07.250
We do it because we know
eventually it'll have bombers

00:27:07.251 --> 00:27:09.230
and that's why the entire team

00:27:09.231 --> 00:27:10.600
just works the extra hours,

00:27:10.601 --> 00:27:12.915
weekends getting things
up. This is great.

00:27:12.916 --> 00:27:15.695
So now, going to
another question which is

00:27:15.696 --> 00:27:18.025
around both of you

00:27:18.026 --> 00:27:20.220
who are very successful
as entrepreneurs,

00:27:20.221 --> 00:27:21.540
and one is from Berkeley,

00:27:21.541 --> 00:27:22.490
the others from Stanford,

00:27:22.491 --> 00:27:23.620
and you're from Berkeley as well.

00:27:23.621 --> 00:27:23.875
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:27:23.876 --> 00:27:25.675
&gt;&gt; Is there something in
the water of the bay area,

00:27:25.676 --> 00:27:27.455
the scarcity of water
in the bay area

00:27:27.456 --> 00:27:29.490
or is there something else

00:27:29.491 --> 00:27:33.410
that makes that schools
there more successful,

00:27:33.411 --> 00:27:36.165
other things that
other schools could learn,

00:27:36.166 --> 00:27:38.860
other than proximity to
the VCs that are there,

00:27:38.861 --> 00:27:40.650
is there something else that

00:27:40.651 --> 00:27:43.630
the university enables
for faculty to

00:27:43.631 --> 00:27:50.055
do more startups of really
scale up startups? So, Dawn?

00:27:50.056 --> 00:27:54.220
&gt;&gt; I think probably the biggest
thing is the ecosystem,

00:27:54.221 --> 00:27:55.690
and assuming the barrier,

00:27:55.691 --> 00:27:57.955
I think there's
a great ecosystem for

00:27:57.956 --> 00:28:01.885
doing startups and also

00:28:01.886 --> 00:28:04.975
it's I guess the mindset

00:28:04.976 --> 00:28:09.700
and the people that you
interact with often,

00:28:09.701 --> 00:28:13.660
they are, I guess
the concentration of

00:28:13.661 --> 00:28:17.770
entrepreneur-minded
people probably is

00:28:17.771 --> 00:28:20.635
fairly high in the bay area,

00:28:20.636 --> 00:28:23.635
and of course it
has a great setup.

00:28:23.636 --> 00:28:28.030
There is the density of
the sea and all that,

00:28:28.031 --> 00:28:30.070
and also Yes, I

00:28:30.071 --> 00:28:32.350
think maybe there's also

00:28:32.351 --> 00:28:34.855
a little bit of a self-selection.

00:28:34.856 --> 00:28:37.825
&gt;&gt; Among the VC's you mean?

00:28:37.826 --> 00:28:40.225
&gt;&gt; I think, again,
that can even be area.

00:28:40.226 --> 00:28:42.835
People who go to the area.

00:28:42.836 --> 00:28:43.600
&gt;&gt; I see.

00:28:43.601 --> 00:28:46.225
&gt;&gt; Also there is bits in
malls and say like tips.

00:28:46.226 --> 00:28:46.900
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:28:46.901 --> 00:28:47.500
&gt;&gt; All right thanks.

00:28:47.501 --> 00:28:49.270
&gt;&gt; But if it's Stanford,

00:28:49.271 --> 00:28:51.885
we see so many of them so what?

00:28:51.886 --> 00:28:52.990
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think so far.

00:28:52.991 --> 00:28:53.825
&gt;&gt; Basically you see a lot too.

00:28:53.826 --> 00:28:54.460
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:54.461 --> 00:28:56.000
&gt;&gt; I was from Berkeley as well.

00:28:56.001 --> 00:28:56.140
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:28:56.141 --> 00:28:57.980
&gt;&gt; We have two Berkeley
people out there.

00:28:57.981 --> 00:28:58.505
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:28:58.506 --> 00:29:00.690
&gt;&gt; You were are
a wealthy person, Yes.

00:29:00.691 --> 00:29:02.830
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but the start
up started from there.

00:29:02.831 --> 00:29:03.920
&gt;&gt; That's true.

00:29:03.921 --> 00:29:08.520
&gt;&gt; Yeah, being in debate is

00:29:08.521 --> 00:29:10.710
definitely good for
connections and also

00:29:10.711 --> 00:29:13.720
for hiring people who've
been out startups and so on.

00:29:13.721 --> 00:29:15.365
It does help with that.

00:29:15.366 --> 00:29:17.200
There are communities
and other cities

00:29:17.201 --> 00:29:19.060
and other area that still
don't know if it can do it.

00:29:19.061 --> 00:29:21.705
So, it can be done but
it definitely helps.

00:29:21.706 --> 00:29:24.180
I think the other thing is having

00:29:24.181 --> 00:29:28.640
a supportive department and
department chair and so on.

00:29:28.641 --> 00:29:30.845
But, I think most universities do

00:29:30.846 --> 00:29:33.675
want to see successful
startups come out of it,

00:29:33.676 --> 00:29:37.010
but you want to make sure
that they let you do it,

00:29:37.011 --> 00:29:39.485
that you're not
penalized in some way.

00:29:39.486 --> 00:29:41.330
&gt;&gt; So, when you say support from

00:29:41.331 --> 00:29:43.025
the department, such as what?

00:29:43.026 --> 00:29:44.640
&gt;&gt; Well, so for example,

00:29:44.641 --> 00:29:47.325
can you go on leave for
some time to do it,

00:29:47.326 --> 00:29:48.770
can you go on partial leave

00:29:48.771 --> 00:29:50.760
maybe because you have
students you want to

00:29:50.761 --> 00:29:52.920
continue supporting them and

00:29:52.921 --> 00:29:54.460
doing research, these
types of things.

00:29:54.461 --> 00:29:57.000
Sometimes it can be
inflexible because

00:29:57.001 --> 00:30:00.165
of policies or
bureaucracy sometimes.

00:30:00.166 --> 00:30:02.550
They'll push through and
make sure if they think it's

00:30:02.551 --> 00:30:05.225
impactful that you can do it.

00:30:05.226 --> 00:30:05.925
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:30:05.926 --> 00:30:09.545
&gt;&gt; Very many Universities
strive to enable that.

00:30:09.546 --> 00:30:11.320
&gt;&gt; Related question.

00:30:11.321 --> 00:30:14.905
So, as big companies as
us here at Microsoft,

00:30:14.906 --> 00:30:17.475
how can we enable

00:30:17.476 --> 00:30:20.310
more such successful
entrepreneurs

00:30:20.311 --> 00:30:22.924
from various universities,

00:30:22.925 --> 00:30:24.470
not just Berkeley and Stanford,

00:30:24.471 --> 00:30:27.000
but universities at
other places as well?

00:30:27.001 --> 00:30:29.640
Would you have any suggestions
on what companies like

00:30:29.641 --> 00:30:32.680
Microsoft can do? Go on.

00:30:32.681 --> 00:30:35.755
&gt;&gt; Okay. As I suppose
giving more money.

00:30:35.756 --> 00:30:39.040
I think that might help.

00:30:39.041 --> 00:30:40.915
I think Microsoft, it's

00:30:40.916 --> 00:30:45.969
just any big company actually
has a lot of advantages,

00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:48.190
that has a lot of
value that they can

00:30:48.191 --> 00:30:50.635
provide to future entrepreneurs,

00:30:50.636 --> 00:30:55.070
to faculty who want to
become entrepreneurs later.

00:30:55.071 --> 00:30:59.170
So for example, Microsoft
also sees a lot of problems.

00:30:59.171 --> 00:31:02.220
So, it knows which problems
at pinpoints to focus and

00:31:02.221 --> 00:31:04.245
which ones may have a big blanket

00:31:04.246 --> 00:31:06.300
that actually need
new technologies,

00:31:06.301 --> 00:31:08.040
and I think Faculty
and Researchers

00:31:08.041 --> 00:31:09.970
are always in love to learn

00:31:09.971 --> 00:31:12.175
about these type problems

00:31:12.176 --> 00:31:15.340
and also Microsoft
has a great platform.

00:31:15.341 --> 00:31:19.270
If anyone develops
a new technology,

00:31:19.271 --> 00:31:23.230
Microsoft offers a great
platform that can introduce

00:31:23.231 --> 00:31:25.165
this technology to a lot of

00:31:25.166 --> 00:31:28.120
users and companies and so on,

00:31:28.121 --> 00:31:31.330
can really help
a new technology to

00:31:31.331 --> 00:31:35.890
be adopted and to grow.

00:31:35.891 --> 00:31:39.970
So, I think that can
be really helpful.

00:31:39.971 --> 00:31:42.490
&gt;&gt; Do you want to add
something to that, Marty?

00:31:42.491 --> 00:31:44.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think beyond
funding which is

00:31:44.481 --> 00:31:46.900
definitely super
important both for

00:31:46.901 --> 00:31:49.640
research actually the areas that

00:31:49.641 --> 00:31:52.900
you think they are
impactful for companies,

00:31:52.901 --> 00:31:56.440
I think Microsoft is
interesting because it has

00:31:56.441 --> 00:32:00.285
wide reach into enterprises
in pretty much any areas,

00:32:00.286 --> 00:32:04.260
any business partnerships with

00:32:04.261 --> 00:32:06.275
startups that will be
impactful and I think those

00:32:06.276 --> 00:32:08.940
already existed in
many areas of Microsoft.

00:32:08.941 --> 00:32:10.250
Maybe people don't know how to

00:32:10.251 --> 00:32:12.790
approach those or how
to get one started,

00:32:12.791 --> 00:32:16.820
but they do make a difference
early on and they can help

00:32:16.821 --> 00:32:18.890
you validate your idea and

00:32:18.891 --> 00:32:21.550
be good basically for
both sides of the partnership.

00:32:21.551 --> 00:32:23.650
&gt;&gt; So, how can Microsoft
approach these?

00:32:23.651 --> 00:32:25.890
So, both of you said that
we are great at reach out,

00:32:25.891 --> 00:32:27.470
but what is
a good medium to reach

00:32:27.471 --> 00:32:30.180
out to these entrepreneurs
to the startup?

00:32:30.181 --> 00:32:32.200
Is it through courses, students,

00:32:32.201 --> 00:32:34.125
do you have any suggestions,

00:32:34.126 --> 00:32:40.610
or how do we engage better
with such budding startups,

00:32:40.611 --> 00:32:44.950
or then maybe co-marketing
events or things like that?

00:32:44.951 --> 00:32:45.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:32:45.581 --> 00:32:47.930
&gt;&gt; Anyway, while we are coming
to the end of the time,

00:32:47.931 --> 00:32:49.770
just any parting
thoughts on faculty

00:32:49.771 --> 00:32:51.610
here who might be thinking

00:32:51.611 --> 00:32:53.330
about taking that idea

00:32:53.331 --> 00:32:56.550
out and scaling it out
through a startup? So, Don.

00:32:56.551 --> 00:32:58.485
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's simple question.

00:32:58.486 --> 00:33:00.635
I think one is that,

00:33:00.636 --> 00:33:04.180
entrepreneurship is
something really fun to try.

00:33:04.181 --> 00:33:07.150
Of course it's also very
high in terms of duty,

00:33:07.151 --> 00:33:16.480
it takes a lot of time and you
have to focus a lot on it,

00:33:16.481 --> 00:33:22.540
and also people do

00:33:22.541 --> 00:33:25.780
read many papers but not
everything on paper is

00:33:25.781 --> 00:33:27.330
necessarily something that you

00:33:27.331 --> 00:33:29.280
may want to turn
it into a startup.

00:33:29.281 --> 00:33:31.500
So typically for startup,

00:33:31.501 --> 00:33:35.070
we want technology
that's both innovative,

00:33:35.071 --> 00:33:38.290
so that gives you an edge
and also you need to

00:33:38.291 --> 00:33:40.270
be really practical enough

00:33:40.271 --> 00:33:42.250
that actually can be
used in the real world.

00:33:42.251 --> 00:33:44.230
So, many papers that
we write about,

00:33:44.231 --> 00:33:48.220
it has new ideas that
really are cool ideas but

00:33:48.221 --> 00:33:49.300
it may still be at

00:33:49.301 --> 00:33:52.060
the very early stage that's
not practical enough yet.

00:33:52.061 --> 00:33:55.390
So those are probably not
ready for doing a startup yet.

00:33:55.391 --> 00:33:57.475
Also I think they need to

00:33:57.476 --> 00:33:59.265
resolve a big enough problem

00:33:59.266 --> 00:34:00.600
that people are
willing to pay for it,

00:34:00.601 --> 00:34:02.385
and has a big enough market

00:34:02.386 --> 00:34:06.360
and I think it as faculty
and researchers who

00:34:06.361 --> 00:34:08.890
are looking at starting

00:34:08.891 --> 00:34:10.600
their company or trying

00:34:10.601 --> 00:34:12.625
to see which technology
that they have developed,

00:34:12.626 --> 00:34:15.895
they may want to take
into the markets,

00:34:15.896 --> 00:34:18.730
that is something
helpful to keep in mind.

00:34:18.731 --> 00:34:20.905
&gt;&gt; Okay. Marty got
something to say?

00:34:20.906 --> 00:34:26.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the reasons to
do are basically impact

00:34:26.171 --> 00:34:28.890
and also ability to
work on problems that

00:34:28.891 --> 00:34:31.845
maybe would be harder
to work on in academia.

00:34:31.846 --> 00:34:35.890
So these are both
good reasons to do it.

00:34:35.891 --> 00:34:38.070
It is a lot of
work, as Dawn said,

00:34:38.071 --> 00:34:39.880
and it's a large commitment

00:34:39.881 --> 00:34:42.350
and it will take
more time than you

00:34:42.351 --> 00:34:45.000
expect even if you take
just a little bit of

00:34:45.001 --> 00:34:47.720
time sabbatical or whatever
and then you want to go back.

00:34:47.721 --> 00:34:49.850
If things are going
well you'll want to

00:34:49.851 --> 00:34:51.280
continue being involved and

00:34:51.281 --> 00:34:53.185
they'll be a lot of
things you can do.

00:34:53.186 --> 00:34:56.680
So, it is a somewhat
large commitment.

00:34:56.681 --> 00:34:58.540
But on the other hand,
you can really see

00:34:58.541 --> 00:35:00.160
things that you
wouldn't see outside,

00:35:00.161 --> 00:35:03.560
and especially if you
find a good team you

00:35:03.561 --> 00:35:05.570
have investors or partners

00:35:05.571 --> 00:35:07.590
who can help get things started,

00:35:07.591 --> 00:35:10.960
they can accelerate that
because it's also their job to

00:35:10.961 --> 00:35:13.250
help you make those connections

00:35:13.251 --> 00:35:15.680
and get those other users.

00:35:15.681 --> 00:35:17.760
&gt;&gt; Thank you Marty and Don,

00:35:17.761 --> 00:35:20.185
and we will in
the bus for dinner.

00:35:20.186 --> 00:35:21.380
Feel free to ask us questions,

00:35:21.381 --> 00:35:23.330
hope you all enjoyed
this session.

00:35:23.331 --> 00:35:24.620
If you have any more
questions, we'd love to

00:35:24.621 --> 00:35:26.575
carry on the discussion
over dinner.

00:35:26.576 --> 00:35:29.270
Thank you everyone. Thank you.

00:35:29.670 --> 00:35:32.060
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

